    "body": "> if one looks at the HTML standard, then the [`<img>` element specification](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#the-img-element) is part of a [section](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/embedded-content.html#embedded-content) entitled… \"4.8 Embedded content\"\r\n\r\nRight, this is why the section makes the distinction between embedded by reference and by direct inclusion, but then we lump both together and say that the svg content restrictions apply to both without clarifying any of the other details.\r\n\r\nI'd like to see the section changed along these lines:\r\n\r\n> [XHTML content documents](https://www.w3.org/TR/epub-33/#dfn-xhtml-content-document) support the embedding of SVG by _reference_ (embedding via reference, for example, from an `img` or `object` element) and by _inclusion_ (embedding via direct inclusion of the `svg` element in the XHTML content document).\r\n> \r\n> SVGs embedded by reference are SVG core media types so their requirements are already defined in [6.2 SVG content documents](https://www.w3.org/TR/epub-33/#sec-svg).\r\n> \r\n> SVGs embedded by inclusion MUST meet the content conformance constraints for [SVG content documents](https://www.w3.org/TR/epub-33/#dfn-svg-content-document) in [6.2.3 Restrictions on SVG](https://www.w3.org/TR/epub-33/#sec-svg-restrictions). Although the `epub:type` attribute is allowed on [HTML] embedded content, its [use is restricted](https://www.w3.org/TR/epub-33/#confreq-svg-structural-semantics) in the same way as for SVG content documents. \r\n>\r\n> NOTE:\r\n> The `prefix` attribute is only allowed on the root `html` element for SVG embedded by inclusion.",
